# Fixed no-replace probe host wrapper (preparation only)

`probe_host.py` is a library with `PROBE_IO_DISABLED = True`. There is no CLI or
environment switch. No enabled probe was built or run for this work; the native
binary currently present is disabled and must never be treated as a passing
live probe. The supplemental device scope still requires separate approval,
an independently reviewed enabled source/build, and its exact binary digest.

Future interface:

`ProbeHost(adapter, artifact, approved_sha256=..., approved_bytes=...).run()`

The material digest and byte length are integrity bindings, **not permission**.
The exact frozen LIVE v3 adapter class/source and transport source are pinned.
The wrapper uses the existing same-device global uncertainty state. It requires
normal transport, not reconciliation or recovery mode. Existing explicit
cancellation and finite phase deadline requirements remain enforced by the
adapter. It never changes either module, its gates, or its read allowlist.

The only extra uploaded path is
`/data/local/tmp/e5-clean-noreplace-probe.<txid>`. Upload is capped at 4 MiB,
uses no-clobber redirection, a 6-second remote timeout, and absolute deadline
checks before receiving and after EOF before chmod 700. The full bytes are
independently read back and hashed twice with exact stat/boot/path/length frames
before the enclosing ACTIVE can complete. Host timeout or lost input does not
prove a remote cat/syscall exited; failure remains UNKNOWN, with no cleanup.

The native call uses the fixed data/lock inode identities and only the three
approved names within the existing lock: `probe-source.<txid>`,
`probe-destination.<txid>`, `probe-renamed.<txid>`. Every native JSON record's
boot, transaction, operation, process identity and deadline is checked, including
the exact ordered phase sequence and success proof fields. Native code observes
the intermediate EEXIST and missing-destination rename. The host independently
observes only the final state: all three names absent, data/local/tmp/lock
identity unchanged, and owner bytes/hash/full metadata unchanged. It also
rechecks the helper. The host receipt is fsynced while ACTIVE is still present.
There is no claim that the host independently witnessed the intermediate rename.
Success requires the host result **and** the matching transport COMPLETE record,
with no ACTIVE/UNKNOWN/UNCERTAIN barrier left by that operation. A result file
alone is insufficient: its persistence can succeed before the later transport
completion fails, in which case the outcome remains UNKNOWN.

Any error, timeout, invalid ACK, readback drift or host persistence failure
poisons the existing transport and forbids retry. No rootfs/B/misc write,
reboot, automatic resume, recursive cleanup or recovery operation is included.
The normal native success path removes only its own verified empty test dirs;
the host issues no removal command. This is not a power-loss safety proof.

`test_probe_host.py` has 18 pure fake/host tests and an audit hook denying all
subprocess and socket calls. It checks the same-ACTIVE callback sequence and
failure boundaries, not Android timing, permission behavior, native execution,
remote signal termination or F2FS durability. Existing v3 transport tests and
native probe tests remain separate evidence. The frozen v3/live sources are not
modified by this wrapper.
